            <ArticleTitle>Prevalence and Nature of Spiritual Distress Among Palliative Care Patients in India.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>530-544</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10943-016-0252-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In palliative care research, little attention has been paid to the empirical study of spirituality in patients in non-Western countries. This study describes the prevalence and nature of spiritual distress among Indian palliative care patients. Data from 300 adult cancer patients who had completed a questionnaire with 36 spirituality items were analyzed. Spirituality was shaped by the Indian religious and economic context. A latent class analysis resulted in three clusters: trustful patients (46.4 %), spiritually distressed patients (17.4 %), and patients clinging to divine support (36.2 %). After regression, the clusters were found to be associated with pain scores (p &lt; .001), gender (p = .034), and educational level (p &lt; .006). More than half of the patients would benefit from spiritual counselling. More research and education on spirituality in Indian palliative care is urgently required.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
